issue.body:
Found this while debugging strange numerical errors in `bf16` e2e matmul tests that causes #15243 to be rolled back.

The results of `bf16` matmuls in these e2e tests appear at first glance to be just garbage, but looking closer, there is a logic to it: the underlying bits of the `bf16` values are just plainly multiplied as `i16` integers, there is no `bf16` aspect at all anymore in the generated code.

Minimized to this testcase:

```mlir
module {
  func.func @foo(%8 : vector<1xbf16>, %5 : vector<1xbf16>, %11 : vector<1x1xbf16>) -> vector<1x1xbf16> {
    %12 = vector.outerproduct %8, %5, %11 {kind = #vector.kind<add>} : vector<1xbf16>, vector<1xbf16>
    return %12 : vector<1x1xbf16>
  }
}
```

Run:

```
tools/iree-opt --iree-convert-bf16-to-uint16-buffers /tmp/z.mlir
```

Result:

```
module {
  func.func @foo(%arg0: vector<1xi16>, %arg1: vector<1xi16>, %arg2: vector<1x1xi16>) -> vector<1x1xi16> {
    %0 = vector.outerproduct %arg0, %arg1, %arg2 {kind = #vector.kind<add>} : vector<1xi16>, vector<1xi16>
    return %0 : vector<1x1xi16>
  }
}
```

@rsuderman WDYT?
